---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CalorieCalculator from '../../../components/CalorieCalculator.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';

const schemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Calorie Calculator',
    url: 'https://tdeecheck.com/calculators/calorie-calculator/',
    applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How many calories do I need per day?',
    a: 'It depends on your size, age, sex, and activity. A moderately active woman often maintains on roughly 2,000 to 2,400 kcal and a moderately active man on roughly 2,500 to 2,900 kcal, but individual numbers vary a lot. Enter your own stats above for a personal estimate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much of a deficit do I need to lose 1 lb a week?',
    a: 'About 500 kcal per day below your maintenance level, because a pound of body fat stores roughly 3,500 kcal. Real world results vary week to week, so judge by the trend over 3 or more weeks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 1,200 calories a day enough?',
    a: 'For most adults it is too low to sustain and can make it hard to cover protein, fiber, and micronutrients. It is a common clinical floor for women under supervision, not a default goal. Smaller deficits reach the same destination with far better adherence.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my calorie target different from my friends?',
    a: 'Because the inputs differ. Height, weight, age, sex, and activity all feed the formula. Two people of the same weight can differ by several hundred calories a day if one is taller, younger, or has a physical job.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Calorie Calculator - Daily Calorie Needs by Goal"
  description="Free calorie calculator that turns your stats and activity into daily calorie targets for losing, maintaining, or gaining weight, with weekly totals."
  path="/calculators/calorie-calculator/"
  schemas={schemas}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Calculators', path: '/calculators/' },
    { name: 'Calorie Calculator', path: '/calculators/calorie-calculator/' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900">Calorie Calculator</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        How many calories should you actually eat? Enter your details, pick a goal, and get a daily target with every
        other goal shown beside it for comparison.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-4xl">
      <CalorieCalculator />
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">How this calculator works</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Behind the scenes it runs the same engine as our
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/">TDEE Calculator</a>: first your BMR with the Mifflin-St Jeor
      formula, then multiplied by your activity level to get your maintenance calories, your TDEE. Your chosen goal then
      adjusts that number. Losing about a pound a week means a 500 kcal daily deficit, gaining slowly means a 250 kcal
      surplus, and so on. Presenting every goal in one table matters, because the right goal is the one you can stick to
      for months, not the most aggressive one.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Choosing a sensible goal</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      If you have a lot to lose and your maintenance level is high, a 500 kcal deficit usually feels manageable. If you
      are already lean, or your maintenance is low, the same 500 kcal cut can mean eating very little food, and a 250 kcal
      deficit is the smarter play. The same logic applies in reverse for gaining: lean people gain better muscle to fat
      ratios on a small surplus. Whatever you pick, give it 3 consistent weeks before you judge it. Weight trends, not
      single days, tell the truth.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Calories are the headline, macros are the detail</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Total calories decide the direction of your weight. Macros decide what that weight change is made of. Enough protein
      protects muscle in a deficit and builds it in a surplus. Once you have your calorie target, split it with the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/macro-calculator/">Macro Calculator</a>, or use the
      homepage tool, which returns calories and macros together for every goal.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 rounded-xl bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-ink-700">
      Reviewed by <strong>Farah Akram, Dietitian</strong>. Estimates only, not medical advice. See the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/medical-disclaimer/">medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
    <Faq faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
